WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
Timeline analysis brings browser artifacts together with other evidence sources.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.620
Browser timestamps may appear in different formats, levels of precision, and meanings depending on browser family, version, database, cache record, or platform.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.640
Before building a timeline, investigators should understand what each timestamp represents and whether the tool converted it correctly.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.660
Time zones and clock drift can create confusion.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:31.240
A browser database may store a value in UTC, local time, WebKit time, Unix time, or another browser-specific format.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.140
A system clock may be wrong.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.960
A virtual machine, remote session, or restored profile may introduce additional context.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.940
The report should preserve original values where appropriate and document how normalization was performed.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:56.920
A profile activity timeline may include history visits, downloads, searches, form entries, cache records, cookies, storage updates, extension activity, tab restore data, and notifications.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.020
But browser-only timelines can overstate certainty.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.120
Many browser artifacts are generated by application behavior rather than direct user selection.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.620
The timeline should distinguish observed records from inferred behavior.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.920
Correlation improves confidence.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:24.260
Browser records can be compared with file system metadata, endpoint telemetry, proxy logs, DNS records, web application logs, email records, cloud audit logs, authentication logs, and user context.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.200
If several sources point to the same sequence, the conclusion becomes stronger.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.980
If sources conflict, the conflict is part of the finding, not something to hide.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.400
Good timeline reporting separates observation from inference.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:46.820
It may say that a profile contains a visit record for a URL at a certain time, a download record for a file shortly after, and an endpoint event showing the file was opened.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.960
It should not jump directly to intent unless the evidence supports it.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.540
Uncertainty notes make timelines more credible, not weaker.
